#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162882} :activity/id #uuid "4407446b-5fc6-5ec1-a901-75a95c2caa7b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048871 :topic/id #uuid "c80985c0-d51a-5f21-9b71-21587d8d82cc" :topic/math-academy-id 3024} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162882 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163239 :tutorial/content "If $X$ is a continuous random variable and\n\n$$\n0 \\leq p \\leq 1\n$$\n\nthen the $100p$th **percentile** is a number $x_p$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq x_p) = p\n$$\n\n- In terms of CDF, the *cumulative distribution function* $F(x)$ of $X$, the $100p$th percentile $x_p$ is a number on the $x$-axis such that\n\n$$\nF(x_p) = p\n$$\n\n- In terms of PDF, the *probability density function* $f(x)$ of $X$, the $100p$th percentile $x_p$ is a number on the $x$-axis such that the area under the graph of $y=f(x)$ to the left of $x$ is equal to $p$:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{x_p} f(x) \\, \\text{d}x = p\n$$\n\nFor example, let's find the $4$th percentile of the random variable $X$ if the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{2}x,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nThe $4$th percentile corresponds to the decimal\n\n$$\np={\\color{red}0.04}\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n100 \\cdot 0.04 = 4\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le x_{0.04}) &= \\int_{0}^{x_{0.04}}\\frac{1}{2}xdx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{x^{2}}{4}\\right|_{0}^{x_{0.04}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{x_{0.04}^{2}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $4$th percentile is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x_{0.04}^{2}}{4} &= 0.04 \\\\\nx_{0.04}^{2} &= 0.16 \\\\\nx_{0.04} &= 0.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x_{0.04} \\leq 2\n$$\n\nThere are a few special percentiles:\n\n- the $25$th percentile is called the **lower quartile**. This is the number $q_1$ such that\n$P(X \\leq q_1) = 0.25$.\n- the $50$th percentile is called the **median**. This is the number $m$ such that\n$P(X \\leq m) = 0.5$.\n- the $75$th percentile is called the **upper quartile**. This is the number $q_3$ such that\n$P(X \\leq q_3) = 0.75$.\n- the **interquartile range** is the difference between the upper and lower quartiles, that is,\n$IQR = q_3 - q_1$." :tutorial/id #uuid "e91993d1-ae51-5742-8eeb-e76348c0b8af" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4942 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b587e6fa-53fb-5f03-9ffa-373892fc4d10" :step/math-academy-id 30817 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162883}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162883 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161997 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161789 :question/id #uuid "dd626772-dd96-5840-8375-9ace79fecf70" :question/math-academy-id "e-8237"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84c1cf7c-e334-5173-8ae4-86af0dce56ab" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050750}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162640}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Median Given the CDF a Continuous Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "6d1c73ec-5860-5de5-9ee3-0fa09198d61f" :step/math-academy-id 30818 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162884}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162884 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161998 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161790 :question/id #uuid "d22e7154-ac3c-5ac6-ad86-77a7eb08f82e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8238"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a79c4207-c5d6-5386-818e-a7e532492732" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049638}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162627}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Median Given the PDF a Continuous Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "3f37a92a-9642-5a0c-8bf3-31a55408c5bf" :step/math-academy-id 30819 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162885}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162885 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161999 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161791 :question/id #uuid "3be25e37-7162-5565-86d2-47ea69c599ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-8239"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "563f7ff5-8ebb-5130-badb-f092fa348959" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050081}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162641}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quartile of a Continuous Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "06e2a627-c14a-552c-af48-980810edbdcf" :step/math-academy-id 30820 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162886}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162886 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162000 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161792 :question/id #uuid "d4d4f78a-05e2-57cf-8630-110daa4b61da" :question/math-academy-id "e-8240"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84b181c4-fc23-5a68-8042-55f02b1e5e8a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162644}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Percentile of a Continuous Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "f08cfe76-d094-5792-86f2-7b599fc240f8" :step/math-academy-id 30821}] :activity/title "Median, Quartiles and Percentiles of Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162178}]}
